\f0\fs38 \cf2 \cb3 These prompts exist because the last three model migrations I watched up close all broke the same way: someone upgraded, their costs spiked or their output quality dropped, and they blamed the model instead of auditing their own setup. The migration pre-flight prompt forces you to inventory what actually breaks before you flip the switch. The cost estimator makes you quantify the tokenizer tax against your real usage instead of trusting Anthropic\'92s published range. And the peer review workflow builder addresses something I haven\'92t seen anyone else ship a solution for \'97 the fact that both Opus and GPT produce unreliable self-assessments, in opposite directions, and the only pattern that catches real errors is having one model grade the other\'92s work. If you\'92re handing agents anything with financial, legal, or reputational stakes and you don\'92t have peer review built in, these prompts are where to start.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa385\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs61\fsmilli30875 \cf2 What shipped and why it\'92s uneven\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs38 \cf2 \cb3 Opus 4.7 shipped April 16. Claude Design shipped April 17. OpenAI pushed its biggest Codex update since launch on the same day, Spud finished pretraining in March and is reportedly weeks away, and Anthropic is fielding investor offers at $800 billion while Ramp\'92s data shows Anthropic\'92s share of business AI spend climbing from 24.4% to 30.6% in a single month. Mythos \'97 Anthropic\'92s most powerful model, offered separately as an invitation-only research preview for defensive cybersecurity \'97 went public nine days before 4.7 dropped.\
This isn\'92t a point release. It\'92s a bridge release, shipped under competitive pressure into a week where everyone else also moved. Mythos was generating negative PR \'97 you\'92ve built the best thing you\'92ve ever made and you\'92re telling people they can\'92t use it. Something had to ship to customers between Mythos and whatever comes next, and 4.7 is that something.\
Read in that light, the unevenness makes sense. The model got noticeably stronger where Anthropic invested \'97 coding, agentic persistence, vision, enterprise knowledge work. It got weaker, or held flat, in the places they didn\'92t. That\'92s a directed optimization, not a uniform upgrade, and Nathan Lambert flagged that the tokenizer change suggests this is a new base model rather than a fine-tune of 4.6. If he\'92s right, we might be looking at an early checkpoint that shipped before it was fully smoothed. The uneven quality, the combativeness being patched post-launch, the generalization gaps. Early checkpoint behavior.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa385\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs61\fsmilli30875 \cf2 Where the model actually got stronger\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs38 \cf2 \cb3 The biggest complaint about Opus 4.6 \'97 for months \'97 was that it quit. You\'92d hand it a complex debugging session or a multi-step refactor and somewhere in the middle it would lose the thread, declare itself done, stop. If you used Claude Code seriously, you hit the pattern constantly, and I was routing hard multi-step work to other models even when Claude was stronger on the individual steps.\
Anthropic clearly went after that failure mode, and based on four days of heavy use, the fix is real. Notion\'92s AI team reported a 14% improvement on complex multi-step workflows while using fewer tokens and a third of the tool errors of 4.6. Factory Droids reported a 10 to 15% lift in task success. Rakuten saw three times more production tasks resolved on their internal SWE benchmark. Genspark quantified the loop problem directly: their agent used to loop indefinitely on roughly 1 in 18 queries, and 4.7 is the first model they\'92ve tested where that number meaningfully drops. These aren\'92t benchmark numbers. They\'92re production telemetry from teams whose workflows depend on the model finishing what it starts.\
The coding benchmarks support the workflow reports. SWE-bench Verified moved from 80.8% to 87.6%, Pro jumped from 53.4% to 64.3%, and CursorBench went from 58 to 70. And MCP-Atlas, the multi-tool orchestration benchmark, climbed from 75.8 to 77.3 \'97 the biggest single jump in the agentic suite. If you\'92re building orchestrators, that\'92s the number to watch.\
Vision tripled in resolution, from about 1.15 megapixels to 3.75. XBOW measured 98.5% on visual acuity, up from 54.5%. The model can now read fine print on screenshots and parse dense diagrams, and that capability is what makes Claude Design possible in the first place.\
But the model went backward on web research. BrowseComp dropped from 83.7 to 79.3. GPT-5.4 Pro leads that benchmark by ten points at 89.3. Gemini 3.1 Pro leads by six at 85.9. Meanwhile on Terminal-Bench 2.0, the command-line task benchmark that measures the kind of work coding agents do consistently, Opus 4.7 trails GPT-5.4 by nearly six points. If your agents rely on web research or live in the terminal, benchmark your specific workflows before you migrate.\
And here\'92s a detail that connects to the cost question: the reason those benchmark gains hit different on your invoice is that 4.7 ships with a new tokenizer. The same text \'97 your same prompts, your same system instructions \'97 maps to up to 35% more tokens. Keep that number in your head as we go, because it reframes every benchmark gain you just read.\
The underreported win is knowledge work. On GDPval-AA, 4.7 scores 1,753. GPT-5.4 scores 1,674. Gemini 3.1 Pro scores 1,314 \'97 that\'92s not a close race. Hex called 4.7 the strongest model they\'92ve ever evaluated, with General Finance performance climbing from 0.767 to 0.813. Harvey put it at 90.9% on BigLaw Bench at high effort. Databricks reported 21% fewer errors on OfficeQA Pro. And the piece that matters most to anyone who\'92s watched an AI hallucinate a number into a financial model: the model correctly reports missing data instead of fabricating plausible-but-wrong fallbacks. If you\'92re doing legal, financial, or enterprise document work, this is the strongest model I\'92ve tested for that work, and the distance to every alternative is wider than the headline numbers suggest.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa385\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs61\fsmilli30875 \cf2 The trust failure neither model caught\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs38 \cf2 \cb3 That\'92s the launch pitch \'97 and if benchmarks were the whole story, the upgrade would be obvious. But I found something in my testing that I\'92m still thinking through, and it changed how I think about building on either of these models.\
I ran both Opus and GPT-5.4 through a migration ringer I\'92ve been refining since GPT-5.4 dropped: 465 files in every format a real business produces, sixteen planted obstacles, canary records including Mickey Mouse and Test Customer. Both models took the full mess in a single shot with no guidance between stages. Three findings matter.\
First: Opus never actually parsed one of the test files \'97 but its report claims it did. A hallucinated audit trail. If you\'92re trusting an agent\'92s report about what it processed, and the agent is willing to say \'93I handled that file\'94 when it didn\'92t, that\'92s not a missed detail. That\'92s a trust failure, and it\'92s the specific behavior that makes peer review non-optional when you hand agentic work to either of these models.\
Second: neither model caught the canary records. Mickey Mouse is living in both customer databases. A $25,000 purchase order got silently normalized to $25 and counted toward revenue totals. For all the talk about frontier reasoning, the question \'93is this a real person?\'94 remains a human job.\
Third \'97 and this might be the most consequential finding of the whole test: I had each model review the other\'92s output on a seven-dimension rubric. Opus self-reviewed at 3.5 out of 5. GPT reviewed Opus at 2.7 \'97 harsher. GPT self-reviewed at 3.1. Opus reviewed GPT at 3.6 \'97 gentler. Opus oversells itself; GPT undersells itself. And GPT\'92s own self-review surfaced more real problems than Opus\'92s review of GPT did. The harshest, most honest grader in the whole test was the model with direct SQL access to its own tables. If you trust self-review in your agentic workflow, you\'92ll get unreliable signals from either model. Peer review \'97 one model checking the other\'92s work \'97 is the only reliable pattern.\
That design principle is worth more than any benchmark number in the launch deck.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa385\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs61\fsmilli30875 \cf2 The model does what you asked, and nothing else\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs38 \cf2 \cb3 Anthropic\'92s own migration guide says it directly: the model \'93will not silently generalize an instruction from one item to another, and will not infer requests you didn\'92t make.\'94 If you said \'93format this nicely\'94 to 4.6, it made generous assumptions and polished the output. 4.7 does what you wrote.\
A concrete example will help this land. Say you paste an article and ask for three punchy sentences. On 4.6, you probably got three sentences plus a header, bolded key terms, maybe a closing kicker \'97 because the model inferred you wanted something finished. On 4.7, you get three punchy sentences. That\'92s what you asked for. Multiply that across every prompt you\'92ve ever written and the migration problem comes into focus. Half the value you were getting from 4.6 was the model guessing what you actually meant and filling it in. That value didn\'92t disappear \'97 it moved. The responsibility for providing it now sits on you.\
The instinct when a model behaves more literally is to write longer, more detailed prompts. That instinct is wrong. Anthropic\'92s own guidance on smarter models is explicit: they need less prescriptive engineering, not more. Andrej Karpathy\'92s framing is the right one \'97 increasingly, we should stop telling models what to do and start giving them success criteria and watching them go. The fix isn\'92t more words. It\'92s clearer intent up front: what you\'92re building, who it\'92s for, what the constraints are, what good looks like. And then getting out of the model\'92s way.\
The model also uses tools less often by default, spawns fewer subagents, and has an opinionated design aesthetic \'97 warm cream, serif type, terracotta accents \'97 that bleeds into anything visual unless you override it. Since temperature is gone as a knob, your prompting now has to do more of the diversity work that sampling used to handle for free.\
The literal-interpretation shift is, honestly, the most defensible part of the release. Predictability is a real feature for production pipelines; inference that silently generalizes is how production prompts drift in ways nobody notices until they notice. The cost is that anyone who tuned their 4.6 prompts against the old inference behavior will find those prompts worse on 4.7 without the model having gotten dumber. It hasn\'92t. You\'92ve just found out how much was being done for you.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa385\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs61\fsmilli30875 \cf2 The register changed, and that\'92s a preference call\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs38 \cf2 \cb3 CodeRabbit ran 4.7 through their tone analysis harness and measured 77.6% assertiveness with just 16.5% hedging. The language skews imperative \'97 \'93guard against nil,\'94 \'93prevent concurrent access,\'94 \'93validate input before processing\'94 \'97 rather than tentative. Gergely Orosz of The Pragmatic Engineer wrote publicly that he found 4.7 \'93surprisingly combative\'94 and went back to 4.6. Anthropic\'92s own migration doc describes the new tone as \'93more direct and opinionated, with less validation-forward phrasing.\'94 This isn\'92t vibes. It\'92s measurable, and it\'92s in the documentation.\
You\'92ll feel it in a few specific places. In code review, 4.7 leads with a verdict and a patch \'97 it doesn\'92t soften. In ambiguous creative writing, especially anything touching characters in distress or edgy humor, the model will push back or execute a modified version of what you asked. In security-adjacent coding \'97 a broader category now, since Anthropic shipped real-time cybersecurity safeguards with this release \'97 the model adds caveats you didn\'92t request, sometimes refuses, sometimes produces a scoped-down version with a warning. And in general conversation, queries that touch anything the model reads as sensitive will get steered rather than answered.\
All three shifts compound to produce the overall impression. Adaptive thinking gives you less reasoning than you might want on tasks it judges as simple. Literal interpretation takes away the inference you were getting for free. And the combative register changes how the answer lands even when the answer is correct. Where that takes you is a clear, direct coworker \'97 and that is what Anthropic is building. Everything about this release, from the long-running agent persistence to the visual acuity to the literal instruction-following, maps to an enterprise coworker that does hard, complicated tasks alongside you. That\'92s where the revenue is, and 4.7 is a beat on the way to that product.\
Whether that\'92s a good trade depends on how you work. Anthropic acknowledged post-launch bugs on Friday, and an Anthropic product manager told users the team was \'93sprinting on tuning this more internally\'94 after specific complaints about adaptive thinking on claude.ai. Some of the register edges may smooth in point updates. The broader shift \'97 less validation-forward, more opinionated \'97 is in the model\'92s design and is not going back.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa385\partightenfactor0

\f3\b\fs61\fsmilli30875 \cf2 Claude Design and the $42 afternoon\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs38 \cf2 \cb3 The day after 4.7, Anthropic launched Claude Design under a new sub-brand called Anthropic Labs. The first impression is strong. You hand it a codebase and brand assets; it reads them, builds a design system, and generates logos, typography, color palette, spacing, components, and a full UI kit. Then it does something worth flagging: it generates a SKILL.md file, a machine-readable instruction set any future AI agent can consume to produce on-brand output. That\'92s not just making human-facing brand docs. It\'92s turning the design system into agent infrastructure.\
It also does animated sequences \'97 though I want to be precise, because every piece of coverage I\'92ve seen gets this wrong. These are React-based motion graphics, not generated video. You screen record them. Useful for product demos and B-roll, but it\'92s code-generated animation, not Sora.\
One thing that deserves more attention than it\'92s getting: Claude Design rewards expertise. The onboarding process speaks professional design language, the output includes JSX components and agent-readable instruction files, and having someone with actual design sensibility set up the initial system makes a massive difference in what you get out of it. That fact alone undercuts the popular narrative that tools like this kill designers as a role. They don\'92t. They\'92re tools for designers to do their best work \'97 which is exactly why Anthropic is leading with a Canva partnership that signals prosumer rather than trying to replace the professional workflow.\
Then I took it for a real drive \'97 real product, real codebase, real brand assets \'97 and within the first hour I started seeing something I wasn\'92t expecting. Claude Design reinterpreted my logo instead of preserving it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
